    "##The initial exploratory analysis reveals a relatively comprehensive e-commerce dataset comprising 99,441 orders, 112,650 order items, 103,886 payment records, 99,224 review records, 99,441 customer records, 3,095 sellers and over one million geolocation records. However, the different tables have different observational units, making careful data integration essential to avoid duplication and inaccurate aggregation.\n",
    "\n",
    "Missing data patterns: Missing values are concentrated in specific operational fields rather than being uniformly distributed across the dataset. For example, 88.3% of review titles and 58.7% of review messages are missing. This suggests that providing written feedback is optional and that missingness is largely structural rather than necessarily indicating poor data quality. Similarly, missing delivery timestamps may reflect orders that have not completed the delivery process, rather than random data loss. Consequently, missing values should be interpreted according to their operational meaning instead of being indiscriminately imputed.\n",
    "\n",
    "Order status distribution: Out of 99,441 orders, 96,478 (approximately 97.0%) were recorded as delivered. The remaining orders fall into categories such as shipped, canceled, unavailable, invoiced and processing. This distribution indicates that the dataset is dominated by completed transactions. Restricting delivery-performance modelling to delivered orders is therefore methodologically appropriate because actual delivery duration and delivery delays can only be established when delivery has occurred. Nevertheless, this restriction introduces potential selection bias because unsuccessful or incomplete transactions are excluded.\n",
    "\n",
    "Customer purchasing behaviour: The dataset contains 96,096 unique customers compared with 99,441 customer records, while only 3.1% of customers have made more than one purchase. This indicates that repeat purchasing is relatively limited within the observation period. From a business perspective, this presents an opportunity to strengthen customer retention, loyalty programmes and repeat-purchase strategies.\n",
    "\n",
    "Distribution and skewness: The price, freight and product-weight variables exhibit substantial positive skewness, with skewness coefficients of 7.9, 5.6 and 3.6 respectively. This indicates that most transactions involve relatively modest values, while a small number of observations contain exceptionally high values. Such distributions can distort statistical summaries based on means and affect distance-based machine-learning algorithms. Logarithmic transformations and robust scaling are therefore appropriate techniques for reducing the influence of extreme observations.\n",
    "\n",
    "Data integrity: The integrity assessment identifies 1,359 instances where carrier handover occurred before approval, 23 cases where delivery preceded carrier handover and eight delivered orders without delivery timestamps. These inconsistencies indicate that some records may contain timestamp errors or operational recording irregularities. Identifying and resolving such anomalies before modelling improves the reliability of subsequent analysis.\n",
    "\n",
    "Temporal coverage: The dataset spans September 2016 to October 2018. This provides sufficient temporal variation for analysing purchasing and delivery patterns, although the relatively short observation window limits the extent to which the findings can be generalised to current e-commerce conditions."
