    "##5.1 Interpretation of temporal data splitting\n",
    "The predictive modelling task uses a temporal validation strategy, where earlier transactions are used for training and later transactions are reserved for testing.\n",
    "This approach is more appropriate than random splitting because it reflects the practical application of predictive analytics: using historical information to forecast future operational outcomes.\n",
    "The dataset is divided into:\n",
    "Training data: 76,661 observations.\n",
    "Internal validation data: 11,500 observations.\n",
    "Testing data: 19,166 observations.\n",
    "The temporal cutoff is 25 May 2018.\n",
    "The late-delivery rate decreases from 8.8% in the training population to 5.3% in the test population. Similarly, average delivery duration declines from 13.5 days to 8.8 days.\n",
    "These differences indicate that the operational environment changed over time. Consequently, model performance should be interpreted in light of temporal variation rather than assuming that the underlying delivery process remained constant.\n",
    "5.2 Classification: Baseline models versus LightGBM\n",
    "The classification models predict whether an order will arrive later than its estimated delivery date using information available at the moment of purchase.\n",
    "The classification results are presented below.\n",
    "Model\tPR-AUC\tROC-AUC\tBrier score\n",
    "Always-on-time baseline\t0.053\t0.500\t—\n",
    "Distance/promised-days rule\t0.071\t0.478\t—\n",
    "Logistic regression: estimated days + distance\t0.129\t0.775\t0.051\n",
    "Logistic regression: all numeric features\t0.106\t0.701\t0.054\n",
    "LightGBM\t0.104\t0.714\t0.050\n",
    "Interpretation of baseline performance\n",
    "The always-on-time baseline achieves a PR-AUC of 0.053, corresponding to the late-delivery prevalence in the test population.\n",
    "This demonstrates that a model predicting the majority class provides limited value for identifying late deliveries.\n",
    "The simple distance-to-promised-days rule achieves a PR-AUC of 0.071, suggesting that geographical distance relative to the promised delivery period contains some predictive information. However, its ROC-AUC of 0.478 indicates that it does not effectively distinguish late from on-time deliveries across the full range of classification thresholds.\n",
    "Interpretation of logistic regression\n",
    "The logistic regression model using estimated delivery days and geographical distance achieves the strongest classification performance, with a PR-AUC of 0.129 and ROC-AUC of 0.775.\n",
    "This indicates that the combination of promised delivery duration and geographical distance provides useful predictive information.\n",
    "Interestingly, incorporating all numeric variables reduces PR-AUC to 0.106 and ROC-AUC to 0.701. This suggests that additional variables do not necessarily improve predictive performance and may introduce noise or less informative relationships.\n",
    "Interpretation of LightGBM\n",
    "The LightGBM classifier achieves a PR-AUC of 0.104, ROC-AUC of 0.714 and Brier score of 0.050.\n",
    "Although the model captures potentially complex relationships, its classification performance does not exceed that of the simpler logistic regression model using estimated delivery days and distance.\n",
    "This is an important finding: greater model complexity does not automatically translate into improved predictive accuracy.\n",
    "The results suggest that the platform's estimated delivery period and geographical distance are particularly informative predictors of delivery risk.\n",
    "5.3 Interpretation of classification threshold and operational performance\n",
    "The classification threshold is selected using an assumed cost ratio in which failing to identify a late order is three times more costly than incorrectly flagging an on-time order.\n",
    "The validation procedure identifies an optimal threshold of 0.27.\n",
    "At this threshold, the LightGBM classifier produces:\n",
    "Precision: 15.8%.\n",
    "Recall: 6.3%.\n",
    "F1-score: 9.0%.\n",
    "The precision indicates that approximately 16 out of every 100 orders flagged by the classifier are actually late.\n",
    "The recall indicates that the model identifies only 6.3% of all late deliveries.\n",
    "This demonstrates that although the model can prioritise some potentially risky orders, it misses a substantial proportion of actual late deliveries.\n",
